WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.703
If you’ve ever wondered what it might take
to upset a microscopist, just ask James—our

00:00:24.703 --> 00:00:28.501
master of microscopes—his feelings about
tardigrade legs.

00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:32.880
Yes, tardigrade legs.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.127
Those chunky, wiggly limbs 
that move their owner

00:00:36.127 --> 00:00:39.194
through meals of moss 
and fields of debris.

00:00:39.194 --> 00:00:44.477
What could possibly be in question when it
comes to tardigrade legs?

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:50.617
Why would such a seemingly mundane detail
of the invisible world around us become a

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:55.260
repeated point of contention in Journey to
the Microcosmos meetings?

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:02.780
The problem lies in the macroscopic world,
specifically in the myriad of stuffed tardigrade

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.563
toys available for purchase

00:01:05.563 --> 00:01:09.422
and the fact that many of them 
seem to have a fact-checking problem.

00:01:09.422 --> 00:01:13.329
Take a moment right now to count the number
of legs you see on this tardigrade.

00:01:15.986 --> 00:01:20.799
You should have hopefully counted four pairs,
making for a grand total of eight squirmy

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:23.936
limbs on our little water bear friend.

00:01:23.936 --> 00:01:30.635
But unfortunately, many tardigrade toy manufacturers
seem to have stopped a little short in their counting.

00:01:30.635 --> 00:01:35.010
While there are eight-legged tardigrades for
sale, you’ll also find plenty of spurious

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.590
ones that have only six legs.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.799
So to this day, if the question of whether
we should produce our own Journey to the Microcosmos

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:48.390
tardigrade merchandise comes up—including
our t-shirts—James is the first to remind

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:53.571
us that it would be an opportunity to correct
the record in some small way.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:59.748
Now if you have also spent your life thinking
of tardigrades as six-legged creatures, don’t

00:01:59.748 --> 00:02:04.350
worry—we have made the same mistake and
have had to correct previous videos where

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.649
we described them as having six legs.

00:02:08.141 --> 00:02:12.300
Now we’re not quite sure what it is about these
creatures that confuses their watchers in

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.560
this way, but we are all in good company
because the question of just how to count

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:21.819
tardigrade legs has been around since their
discovery.

00:02:22.474 --> 00:02:26.660
And it’s not the only quality of tardigrades
that has inspired disagreement.

00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.000
This creature has been confounding us for
centuries.

00:02:30.464 --> 00:02:34.117
And while sometimes the scientific stakes
have been low, at other times,

00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:36.000
they have been much much higher.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.730
The tardigrade was first described by Johann
August Ephraim Goeze, a preacher in his 40s

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:46.080
who had only just begun his microscopy hobby.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:52.250
But the beauty of the microcosmos is that
it can reveal itself to anyone willing to

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.129
look, regardless of experience.

00:02:55.129 --> 00:03:01.414
And in 1773, Goeze would put the experience
of watching a tardigrade into words

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:02.467
for the first time.

00:03:03.172 --> 00:03:04.277
He said,

00:03:04.277 --> 00:03:10.040
Strange is this little creature, because the
whole organisation of his body is extraordinary

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:16.569
and strange and because his external appearance,
at the first sight, has the closest similarity

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:17.785
to a little bear.

00:03:18.969 --> 00:03:24.640
Goeze called his strange creature “kleinen
Wasserbärs” or “little water bear,”

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.541
a name that has stuck as a nickname for the
tardigrade.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:33.870
And about his little water bear—which he
also at times called a worm—he said,

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:39.680
The most remarkable feature of this little
worm are the eight short feet, each of which

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:43.938
is armed with three crooked and very sharp
claws.

00:03:44.807 --> 00:03:49.060
But while Goeze’s publication was the first
to describe a tardigrade, it would not be

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:50.930
the only one for long.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:56.760
Within a few years, three other scientists
would document their observations and inferences

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:02.620
on the unusual creatures found swimming around
in duckweeds and rain gutters.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.810
Those scientists Johann Conrad Eichhorn, 
Bonaventura Corti, and Lazzaro Spallanzani.

00:04:09.704 --> 00:04:13.612
These papers were likely written independently
of one another.

00:04:13.612 --> 00:04:19.660
And some of the features they described didn’t
line up—particularly the legs.

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:25.510
Eichhorn drew a figure with ten legs, though
we don’t know where he got ten from.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.340
Meanwhile, Spallanzani described a creature
with six legs, though that seems to be due

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.350
to the fact that he considered the last pair
of tardigrade legs to not be legs at all,

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.300
but instead of a pair of hooked filaments.

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.010
Of course, a few mistakes here or there do not
prevent one from making important contributions

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.790
to our collective knowledge.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:49.449
And one of the most lasting contributions
of Spallanzani was the fact that he called

00:04:49.449 --> 00:04:55.870
these creatures “il tardigrado,” which
translates to “sluggard” or “slow walker.”

00:04:55.870 --> 00:05:00.000
Of course, that name has since shifted into
what we now call “tardigrade.”

00:05:03.836 --> 00:05:09.000
It’s not quite clear to us how the consensus
that tardigrades have eight legs was settled.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:14.800
In 1785, a posthumous publication written
by the Danish naturalist Otto Friedrich Müller

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.509
established the correct number as eight.

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:22.110
And a translator of Spallanzani’s work—a
man named John Graham Dalyell—wrote in the

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:26.390
footnotes of his translation about a species
he’d found himself and named

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:31.740
Tardigradus Octopdalis, better known as 
“the tardigrade with eight legs.”

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:36.860
So at some point, toy manufacturers aside,
there was an agreement that tardigrades have

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:38.180
eight legs.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:39.180
Not six.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:40.180
Not ten.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:40.853
Eight.

00:05:40.853 --> 00:05:47.863
It appears to have been a relatively low stakes
and low key argument in the world of tardigradology.

00:05:47.863 --> 00:05:52.672
After all if you can just look at them and be like,
see, count, there's eight.

00:05:52.672 --> 00:05:55.414
Then, it's fairly easy to settle the argument.

00:05:55.414 --> 00:05:57.819
But not all arguments have been that way though.

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:04.780
In the middle of the 19th century, a 156 page
report was written by Pierre Paul Broca, a

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:09.169
French physician best known for his research
on the brain.

00:06:09.169 --> 00:06:15.729
So why was Broca writing about an eight-legged,
slow-walking microscopic creature?

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:22.120
Well, he was there to settle an argument—an
old one—that gets to the core of tardigrades

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:24.780
and their remarkable capacity to survive.

00:06:25.196 --> 00:06:30.530
Today, we know that water bears and many other
microscopic animals are able to survive extreme

00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:37.949
conditions of heat or dryness by draining
themselves of water, desiccating into a husk

00:06:37.949 --> 00:06:43.610
that essentially puts their life on pause
until conditions become tolerable again.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:47.509
We know this process today as anhydrobiosis.

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:53.540
And it was first described in tardigrades
by Bonaventura Corti, who had observed rotifers

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:56.729
and other creatures going through it as well.

00:06:56.729 --> 00:07:02.720
But as more scientists documented the anhydrobiosis
of tardigrades, more questions began to form

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:06.131
around just what anhydrobiosis was.

00:07:06.131 --> 00:07:07.736
Was it death?

00:07:07.736 --> 00:07:09.990
A state of pause?

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:15.680
To answer that, tardigradologists had to agree
on something even more fundamental:

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:22.110
could tardigrades and their fellow dried out
creatures live past anhydrobiosis?

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:28.930
After all, logic and experience suggest that
voiding oneself entirely of moisture is not

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.590
a sound survival strategy.

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:36.410
On the one hand, there were experiments carried
out by Spallanzani and others that seemed

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:41.389
to demonstrate the conditions necessary to
make anhydrobiosis a state that tardigrades

00:07:41.389 --> 00:07:43.660
could resuscitate themselves from.

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:48.480
But others did not believe this kind of resurrection
was possible.

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.920
And in their quest to explain what others
were seeing, some very creative alternative

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:55.244
explanations popped up.

00:07:55.244 --> 00:08:01.319
For example, the eminent naturalist Christian
Gottfried Ehrenberg believed that people

00:08:01.319 --> 00:08:05.840
were actually seeing were the descendants
of the dried out animal emerging from the

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:09.000
husk of their great-great-grandparent.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:14.602
And in 1859, this led to the involvement of
the Société de Biologie in Paris.

00:08:14.602 --> 00:08:20.686
A commission was called to settle the debate
as presented by two French scientists.

00:08:20.686 --> 00:08:25.949
On one side was Félix Archimède Pouchet,
who believed that tardigrades could not be

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:28.555
revived from anhydrobiosis.

00:08:28.555 --> 00:08:34.470
On the other side was Louis Michel François
Doyère, who had documented his tardigrade

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:40.379
resurrection experiments in his dissertation
titled “Memoire sur les Tardigrades.”

00:08:40.379 --> 00:08:44.120
I'm sure you're all so happy to hear my
French pronunciation.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:49.149
Both sides documented their experiments, and
the commission performed control experiments,

00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:56.112
resulting eventually in the report written 
by Paul Broca that stated that Doyère was correct:

00:08:56.112 --> 00:09:03.305
tardigrades that go through anhydrobiosis
can, in fact, be brought back to life from that state.

00:09:03.305 --> 00:09:09.460
Of course, settling this debate only opened
the door for more—for questions that we are

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:15.550
still trying to answer about the mechanisms
that allow tardigrades to survive anhydrobiosis

00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:18.302
and the limits to their resilience.

00:09:18.302 --> 00:09:24.480
But that is the joy of science, the way that
disagreements compel us to strengthen our

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:29.676
own arguments, to seek out the tools and 
experiments that will help us better understand

00:09:29.676 --> 00:09:31.607
the world around us.

00:09:31.607 --> 00:09:36.990
And perhaps, on that note, we should end with
something that we hope we can all agree on

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:40.180
as fellow hitchhikers through the microcosmos.

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:46.860
It’s a note from Goeze’s 1773 paper where
he first described the little water bears

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:48.658
that he had found.

00:09:48.658 --> 00:09:54.871
Surely, there is no greater pleasure than
to see the polyps, the rotifers, and other

00:09:54.871 --> 00:10:01.336
beasts of prey under the magnifying glass
seizing and swallowing their prey.

00:10:01.336 --> 00:10:06.839
This can be achieved without traveling long
distances, without risking one’s life, and

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:08.797
without big costs.

00:10:08.797 --> 00:10:17.160
A glass lens shows us a new world, and I know
from experience that you will be full of amazement,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.218
when seeing that for the first time.

00:10:25.226 --> 00:10:30.739
Thank you for coming on this journey with us 
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:30.739 --> 00:10:35.310
We love diving into history here on Journey
to the Microcosmos, but it’s important to

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:39.430
remember that “History” is not just the
same thing as the past.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:45.898
The past is everything that has ever happened,
history is the curated story of all the important events.

00:10:45.898 --> 00:10:50.080
But who gets to choose what’s “important”
and what influences that decision?

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:56.899
Well, “Study Hall: US history to 1865”
seeks to explore these questions as they cover

00:10:56.899 --> 00:11:01.110
American History from the earliest Indigenous
groups to the Civil War.

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.810
So, if you’re sitting there, craving a bit
more history today, you can head on over to

00:11:04.810 --> 00:11:11.685
youtube.com/studyhall or the link in the description
to watch “Study Hall: US history to 1865”

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:17.769
The people on the screen right now, they are
our patrons on Patreon, and we're so grateful

00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:23.149
that they give us the opportunity to dive
deep and learn about not just what we know

00:11:23.149 --> 00:11:25.799
now but how we came to know it.

00:11:25.799 --> 00:11:30.200
That for me, is the most interesting and more
special thing about science.

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:33.950
Which is not, a body of information that just
appeared somewhere.

00:11:33.950 --> 00:11:38.200
It is all stuff that human beings figured
out.

00:11:38.200 --> 00:11:42.911
So thank you so much to all the people on
the screen right now, for helping us tell these stories.

00:11:42.911 --> 00:11:45.742
If you want to see me from our 
Master of Microscopes, James Weiss,

00:11:45.742 --> 00:11:48.340
you can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:11:48.340 --> 00:11:52.279
And if you want to see more from us, there's
always subscribe button somewhere nearby.

